  "summary": "Berkeleyside's voter guide for the Berkeley City Council District 1 race profiles five candidates on housing, homelessness, the Hopkins Street bike lane, and police surveillance. On Flock ALPR cameras, three candidates (Wrubel, Gangopadhyay, Babitt) said they would vote to end the city's contract, Kromer said he would 'probably' vote against it, and Bernet expressed openness to ALPRs but preferred a vendor other than Flock. The article recaps that the council renewed Berkeley's Flock contract this spring by a one-vote majority and rejected a broader surveillance expansion.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "When the City Council voted this spring to renew Berkeley's contract with the firm Flock Safety to operate automated license plate reader cameras, Kesarwani was part of the single-vote majority that approved the one-year contract.",
      "paragraph_idx": 48
    },
    {
      "quote": "Wrubel, Gangopadhyay and Babitt each said they would vote to end the city's contract with Flock, which they described as a bad actor that cannot be trusted to safeguard data it collects in Berkeley.",
      "paragraph_idx": 49
    },
    {
      "quote": "they have signed a pledge from the group Berkeley Against Mass Surveillance stating they will work to remove Flock devices and never vote to 'approve, renew, expand, or otherwise allow any mass surveillance system.'",
      "paragraph_idx": 53
    },
    {
      "quote": "These cameras don't prevent crime, it doesn't make sense for a city like Berkeley and is inconsistent with our sanctuary city policy.",
      "paragraph_idx": 54
    }
  ],
